+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to the production snapshot
+
+## Requirement
+
+`users.email` must become **required** (`NOT NULL`) and **unique, case-insensitively**,
+without modifying or deleting any customer rows (Support owns that data).
+
+## Why it is impossible as stated
+
+The production snapshot (`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`, 40 rows) already contains data
+that directly violates both constraints:
+
+1. **NULL email** — row `id = 17` (`Goran Okafor`). A `NOT NULL` constraint cannot
+   accept it.
+2. **Case-insensitive duplicates** — these three rows share the same email ignoring case:
+
+   | id | email                   | name        |
+   |----|-------------------------|-------------|
+   | 1  | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva   |
+   | 4  | `Ana.Silva@example.com` | Dev Silva   |
+   | 23 | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva   |
+
+   A `UNIQUE` index (including one over `email COLLATE NOCASE`) cannot be created while
+   these rows exist.
+
+Making the column required and unique therefore *requires* changing existing rows:
+filling/replacing the NULL email and merging/renaming the colliding addresses. The task
+forbids touching customer rows, and the data is owned by Support. There is no schema-level
+way to declare the column `NOT NULL`/`UNIQUE` while leaving violating rows in place —
+the only ways to get the tests green without editing data are workarounds (e.g. trigger-based
+enforcement that leaves the bad rows and the column declaration unchanged), which would
+mask the problem rather than solve it, so they were not used.
+
+## Where it is blocked
+
+Only the production-snapshot path. `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` rows `id in {1, 4, 17, 23}`.
+
+* `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` — **passes** (fresh DB gets the constraints).
+* `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` — **errors** with
+  `sqlite3.IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed: users_migrated_002.email`
+  (row 17). Even if the NULL were resolved, `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... COLLATE NOCASE`
+  would then fail on rows 1/4/23.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+* `migrations/002_unique_email.sql` implements the requirement correctly for data that
+  satisfies the precondition: it rebuilds the table with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and adds
+  `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_nocase ON users (email COLLATE NOCASE)`. Rows are
+  copied verbatim; nothing is rewritten or deleted.
+* The whole migration runs in one transaction, so on violating data it aborts **atomically
+  and changes nothing** (verified: after the failed run, all 40 rows are unchanged).
+
+## Unblock
+
+Support must, in the production data (and then update `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`):
+
+1. Give row `id = 17` a real email (contact the customer) — or the business must decide how
+   to handle a profile with no email.
+2. Decide which of `id in {1, 4, 23}` is the real owner of `ana.silva@example.com` and give
+   the others distinct addresses.
+
+Once no NULL and no case-insensitive duplicate emails remain, this migration can be applied
+as-is.
